# Compute Ecosystem

P1 runs its own clusters, and we point the way to the rest. The Danish compute landscape is
bigger than P1 — as a consortium we know it well, and we help researchers find the resource that
fits, whether it is at P1 or elsewhere.

**We consult and guide. We don't broker access.** Getting onto the systems below goes through
their own institutions and application processes — but if you are unsure where your project
belongs, [talk to us](/about/) first.

## University HPCs

Most Danish universities operate their own HPC facilities, generally available to their own
researchers and collaborators:

- **UCPH** — SCIENCE HPC Centre, Hendrix
- **DTU** — DCC/G-Bar Central, Niflheim, Sofia, Steno, plus 5 department systems
- **SDU** — [UCloud](https://cloud.sdu.dk/), Bitten
- **Aarhus** — [GenomeDK](https://genome.au.dk/)

## National HPCs

- [DeiC](https://www.deic.dk/) — Danish e-Infrastructure Consortium, coordinating national HPC
  resources and granting compute allocations to researchers at Danish universities
- [Computerome](https://computerome.dk/) — the Danish national supercomputer for life sciences,
  with support for sensitive data

## International HPCs

- [LUMI](https://www.lumi-supercomputer.eu/) (Finland, EU) — one of Europe's largest
  supercomputers, accessible to Danish researchers through DeiC allocations

## Where P1 fits

P1's own clusters are the entry point for P1-affiliated researchers: start on the
[DTU HPC](/clusters/dtu/) for general work, the [NGC HPC](/clusters/ngc/) for sensitive data, and
the [Gefion sandbox](/clusters/gefion/) as a springboard to scale. When your needs outgrow them,
the systems above are the next step — and we are happy to help you choose.

